1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,686
The mistake was not his. That should be said at the
start, because Albert Wesker made

2
00:00:13,706 --> 00:00:19,870
very few, and this one belonged to a Swiss security
contractor who had upgraded a camera.

3
00:00:20,269 --> 00:00:25,981
On the night of the fourteenth of August, a courier
drum of Umbrella clinical material

4
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:32,045
— samples from a trial that Halvorsen Pike was running
under contract, and had been quietly

5
00:00:32,065 --> 00:00:37,109
running eleven extra assays on — was due to be
collected from a loading bay.

6
00:00:37,509 --> 00:00:43,750
Wesker collected it himself. He was in Basel for two
days of meetings; the bay was four

7
00:00:43,770 --> 00:00:50,226
minutes from his hotel; and there are certain things
that a man who can cross forty metres

8
00:00:50,246 --> 00:00:54,759
in a hundred and sixtieth of a second
simply does not delegate.

9
00:00:55,159 --> 00:01:00,606
What he did not know was that the bay's camera had
been replaced in June with a model

10
00:01:00,626 --> 00:01:04,079
that records at two hundred and
forty frames a second.

11
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:08,550
Frame 4,411: an empty bay.

12
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:16,389
Frame 4,412: a man in a long coat standing beside
the drum, thirty-one metres from the

13
00:01:16,409 --> 00:01:21,940
door he entered by, in one
two-hundred-and-fortieth of a second.

14
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:28,170
Frame 4,413: the bay is empty and
the drum is gone.

15
00:01:28,569 --> 00:01:34,607
Three frames. Twelve milliseconds. A Swiss night
supervisor reviewing an unrelated alarm

16
00:01:34,627 --> 00:01:40,801
found it on the Thursday, watched it four times, and
sent it to his manager with a message

17
00:01:40,821 --> 00:01:45,619
that said, in German, *look at this and tell
me the camera is broken.*

18
00:01:46,019 --> 00:01:48,170
By Saturday it was on the internet.

19
00:01:48,569 --> 00:01:54,572
Catherine Adeyemi had been awake for twenty-two hours
when she came into his office with

20
00:01:54,592 --> 00:02:00,526
the laptop, and she had already spoken to four
lawyers, two crisis-communications firms

21
00:02:00,546 --> 00:02:06,480
and a man in London whose entire profession is the
removal of things from the internet.

22
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,882
It's on nine platforms. Two million views. The Swiss
contractor has been extremely cooperative

23
00:02:11,902 --> 00:02:16,627
— the original file is with us, the camera has been
replaced, and the supervisor has signed

24
00:02:16,647 --> 00:02:21,112
a very comprehensive agreement. We can have the
copies down in about thirty-six hours,

25
00:02:21,132 --> 00:02:25,545
I think, most of them, and my people say the
forensic community will call it a sensor

26
00:02:25,565 --> 00:02:27,683
artefact within a week if we say nothing.

27
00:02:28,084 --> 00:02:32,313
Wesker watched the three frames. Then
he watched them again.

28
00:02:32,834 --> 00:02:35,913
Who is the largest motion-capture
company in the world?

29
00:02:36,313 --> 00:02:37,243
I'm sorry?

30
00:02:37,644 --> 00:02:40,758
Visual effects. Motion capture. Who
is the best of them?

31
00:02:41,158 --> 00:02:44,466
Kestrel, in Wellington. They did the —
Albert, what are you doing?

32
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:51,001
And Wesker leaned back in the chair and did the
thing that nobody — not Catherine, not

33
00:02:51,021 --> 00:02:57,370
Pell, not the four lawyers, not one of the thirteen
analysts who spent the following year

34
00:02:57,390 --> 00:02:59,516
writing about it — saw coming.

35
00:03:00,037 --> 00:03:04,806
Take it down from nowhere. Buy Kestrel. Nine
hundred million; they will take six. And

36
00:03:04,826 --> 00:03:09,651
put out a statement this afternoon apologising for
the leak of an unfinished marketing

37
00:03:09,671 --> 00:03:09,990
asset.

38
00:03:10,664 --> 00:03:12,334
You're going to say you made it.

39
00:03:12,735 --> 00:03:17,653
I am going to say we *commissioned* it. Badly. For a
campaign about rapid diagnosis, which

40
00:03:17,673 --> 00:03:22,481
is not ready, and which has embarrassed us, and for
which the chief executive of our new

41
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:24,730
visual effects subsidiary has apologised.

42
00:03:25,131 --> 00:03:29,932
That's — the timestamp is real. The camera is real.
There is a Swiss security company

43
00:03:29,952 --> 00:03:32,314
that will confirm every part of the chain.

44
00:03:32,715 --> 00:03:37,929
Yes. And in eleven days, when an independent analyst
confirms that the file is unaltered

45
00:03:37,949 --> 00:03:42,984
and the camera is genuine, we will confirm it too,
and we will explain that the asset

46
00:03:43,004 --> 00:03:48,159
was shot on location using our own site and our own
camera precisely because the agency

47
00:03:48,179 --> 00:03:53,333
wanted it to look authentic, and that this is why it
was so convincing, and that we are

48
00:03:53,353 --> 00:03:53,987
very sorry.

49
00:03:54,388 --> 00:04:00,891
The narrator will now explain why this worked, because
it is the most elegant thing Albert

50
00:04:00,911 --> 00:04:05,457
Wesker ever did and it took him about
ninety seconds to invent.

51
00:04:05,858 --> 00:04:11,886
A lie can be disproved. A cover-up can be exposed.
But a *confession to a lesser crime*

52
00:04:11,906 --> 00:04:18,282
cannot be disproved at all, because everything that
would prove the real thing — the genuine

53
00:04:18,302 --> 00:04:24,817
file, the genuine camera, the genuine unedited
timestamps — is now evidence of the confession.

54
00:04:25,218 --> 00:04:31,120
He did not hide the truth. He handed it to the
world, labelled as a hoax, and let every

55
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:35,068
honest investigator on the planet
authenticate it for him.

56
00:04:35,468 --> 00:04:40,788
And then, three months later, he did it again on
purpose, in front of eleven thousand

57
00:04:40,808 --> 00:04:41,228
people.

58
00:04:41,627 --> 00:04:47,617
The occasion was a product launch. The device being
launched was genuinely important —

59
00:04:47,637 --> 00:04:53,766
a field-deployable version of the assay,
battery-powered, for use in places with no cold

60
00:04:53,786 --> 00:04:58,658
chain — and the launch had been built around
a demonstration of speed.

61
00:04:59,057 --> 00:05:02,807
Twenty minutes in, Wesker stopped
the presentation.

62
00:05:03,327 --> 00:05:07,468
There is a rumour about this company that I would
like to address, because it has become

63
00:05:07,488 --> 00:05:07,893
tiresome.

64
00:05:08,293 --> 00:05:14,922
On the screen behind him, three frames of
black-and-white security footage from a loading

65
00:05:14,942 --> 00:05:15,894
bay in Basel.

66
00:05:16,413 --> 00:05:21,557
Two hundred million people have now watched twelve
milliseconds of video from one of our

67
00:05:21,577 --> 00:05:26,603
sites in Switzerland. It was a marketing asset. We
have apologised for it. A number of

68
00:05:26,623 --> 00:05:28,421
people have decided it was not.

69
00:05:28,701 --> 00:05:29,913
So let us settle it.

70
00:05:30,312 --> 00:05:35,840
He walked to the edge of the stage. He indicated a
steel case on a plinth, twenty-six

71
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:40,802
metres away across the front of the hall,
beside the second camera position.

72
00:05:41,322 --> 00:05:45,916
That case contains the device we are launching. In a
moment I am going to ask our partners

73
00:05:45,936 --> 00:05:50,581
at Kestrel to run the same effect live, because it
turns out that this is what nine hundred

74
00:05:50,601 --> 00:05:51,658
million dollars buys.

75
00:05:52,499 --> 00:05:53,889
He was at the plinth.

76
00:05:54,288 --> 00:05:59,766
No cut. No edit. Eleven thousand people in the hall
and four hundred cameras, and the

77
00:05:59,786 --> 00:06:05,457
man who had been standing at the lectern was standing
at a plinth twenty-six metres away

78
00:06:05,477 --> 00:06:11,019
with a steel case in his hands and the sentence he
had been speaking was not finished.

79
00:06:11,538 --> 00:06:15,002
— and this, ladies and gentlemen, is
our new field unit.

80
00:06:15,401 --> 00:06:21,700
The hall came apart. It is one of the great pieces of
footage of the decade: eleven thousand

81
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:28,019
people on their feet, half of them laughing, all of
them filming, and a man in black glasses

82
00:06:28,039 --> 00:06:33,651
holding up a diagnostic device with the faintest
possible expression of amusement.

83
00:06:34,052 --> 00:06:40,069
The Kestrel chief executive — who had been bought in
August, who had been briefed in October,

84
00:06:40,089 --> 00:06:45,718
who understood exactly what he had been purchased
for and had decided that nine hundred

85
00:06:45,738 --> 00:06:51,366
million dollars was sufficient compensation for a
lifetime of not being believed — gave

86
00:06:51,386 --> 00:06:54,936
four interviews that night about
real-time compositing.

87
00:06:55,336 --> 00:07:01,095
There is no compositing. There is no edit. Every
frame of that footage is real, and it

88
00:07:01,115 --> 00:07:06,874
has been examined for sixty years by people who have
concluded, over and over, that it

89
00:07:06,894 --> 00:07:09,764
is the finest practical effect
ever staged.

90
00:07:10,913 --> 00:07:18,787
Hidden mission complete. Hide in the light. The
truth has been published at the largest

91
00:07:18,807 --> 00:07:26,771
available scale and is now unbelievable. Reward:
Mundane Immunity. Disease, toxin, venom

92
00:07:26,791 --> 00:07:30,763
and ordinary poison have no
purchase on you.

93
00:07:31,353 --> 00:07:34,743
From somewhere behind his eyes, the
passenger spoke.

94
00:07:35,262 --> 00:07:38,317
You told them what you are, and
they applauded.

95
00:07:38,716 --> 00:07:43,636
I told them *precisely* what I am, at the largest
scale available, and it cost me nine

96
00:07:43,656 --> 00:07:48,517
hundred million dollars, and it is the single
cheapest piece of security this company

97
00:07:48,537 --> 00:07:53,744
will ever buy. There is now no footage of me that can
ever be used as evidence of anything.

98
00:07:54,142 --> 00:07:59,376
There was one person on the planet who was not fooled,
and she had not watched the footage

99
00:07:59,396 --> 00:08:01,653
for entertainment. She had measured it.

100
00:08:02,052 --> 00:08:08,465
Dr. Evelyn Marsh had been alone with an archive for
three years. She had built Aeterna

101
00:08:08,485 --> 00:08:14,973
out of a vial labelled *ODD*. She was, by 2029, the
second-best biologist in the world,

102
00:08:14,993 --> 00:08:21,556
and she had spent four evenings with the Singapore
footage, a stopwatch, a frame counter

103
00:08:21,576 --> 00:08:24,623
and the published floor plan of the hall.

104
00:08:25,142 --> 00:08:29,982
Eighteen point four metres in under one frame at
sixty. That is a floor speed above six

105
00:08:30,002 --> 00:08:35,009
hundred kilometres an hour, from a standing start, on
a wooden stage, and you were holding

106
00:08:35,029 --> 00:08:39,812
a case when you arrived, which means you took it off
the plinth *during* the movement.

107
00:08:40,212 --> 00:08:44,543
She was not accusing him. Her hands were
shaking, and it was not fear.

108
00:08:45,062 --> 00:08:50,116
The acceleration alone should have separated your
retinas. The air displacement should

109
00:08:50,136 --> 00:08:55,543
have knocked over the water glass on the lectern, which
it did, which nobody noticed because

110
00:08:55,563 --> 00:09:00,794
everyone was looking at you. Your body is not doing
an unusual thing well, Director. Your

111
00:09:00,814 --> 00:09:03,213
body is doing an impossible thing easily.

112
00:09:03,998 --> 00:09:10,075
Albert Wesker looked at her for a long moment. He had
known this conversation was coming

113
00:09:10,095 --> 00:09:16,172
since 2027. He had decided what he was going to do
about it in about four minutes, three

114
00:09:16,192 --> 00:09:19,498
years ago, and had never
revisited the decision.

115
00:09:19,898 --> 00:09:21,388
He took off the glasses.

116
00:09:22,337 --> 00:09:28,436
Evelyn Marsh did not step back. That is the thing.
Nine people out of ten step back, and

117
00:09:28,456 --> 00:09:32,747
one of the nine faints, and the tenth
reaches for a telephone.

118
00:09:33,148 --> 00:09:35,458
Dr. Evelyn Marsh leaned *in*.

119
00:09:35,977 --> 00:09:41,148
Oh — oh, that's not a mutation. That's an
infection. That's an infection that *won*.

120
00:09:41,548 --> 00:09:41,946
Yes.

121
00:09:42,346 --> 00:09:47,739
The pupil geometry — you have a nictitating
response, you have a tapetum, those are not

122
00:09:47,759 --> 00:09:53,215
human structures, they are not even mammalian in that
configuration, what is the vector,

123
00:09:53,235 --> 00:09:56,016
is it integrating, is it
episomal, how long —

124
00:09:56,416 --> 00:09:57,511
Thirty-one years.

125
00:09:57,910 --> 00:09:58,871
You're thirty-eight.

126
00:09:59,272 --> 00:10:03,635
I am considerably older than thirty-eight, and I
have been carrying this since before

127
00:10:03,655 --> 00:10:07,762
you were born, in a place you would not believe
if I told you, which I will not.

128
00:10:08,162 --> 00:10:13,975
She sat down on a laboratory stool, very slowly, in a
room ninety feet under a car park,

129
00:10:13,995 --> 00:10:19,676
and she looked up at him with an expression that the
narrator has been dreading having

130
00:10:19,696 --> 00:10:20,472
to describe.

131
00:10:20,871 --> 00:10:26,444
It was not fear. It was not horror. It was the face
of a person who has spent her whole

132
00:10:26,464 --> 00:10:32,101
life suspecting that the world is smaller than it
should be, and has just been shown the

133
00:10:32,121 --> 00:10:33,451
edge of the real one.

134
00:10:33,971 --> 00:10:34,826
Can I have it?

135
00:10:35,886 --> 00:10:36,029
No.

136
00:10:36,429 --> 00:10:37,131
Why not?

137
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:42,231
Because it would kill you. Nine hundred and ninety-nine
thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine

138
00:10:42,251 --> 00:10:46,698
people out of a million die of this, Dr. Marsh, and
the millionth becomes something that

139
00:10:46,718 --> 00:10:51,012
is no longer interested in what it used to want. I
am not being kind to you; kindness

140
00:10:51,032 --> 00:10:55,529
is not a service I offer. You are the second-best
biologist alive and I have four decades

141
00:10:55,549 --> 00:10:56,342
of work for you.

142
00:10:56,743 --> 00:10:57,873
And when I'm sixty?

143
00:10:58,273 --> 00:11:02,540
Then you will have spent thirty years making a
version that does not kill the person it

144
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:06,828
improves, and I will be standing there when you take
it, and it will be the first thing

145
00:11:06,848 --> 00:11:10,326
in the history of this planet that was
*designed* rather than survived.

146
00:11:10,726 --> 00:11:16,860
The narrator notes, for the record, that this is
the exact conversation that produces

147
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:23,087
the Matriarch in 2036, that Wesker knew it while he
was having it, and that he did not

148
00:11:23,107 --> 00:11:24,896
consider it manipulation.

149
00:11:25,297 --> 00:11:27,017
He considered it recruitment.

150
00:11:28,167 --> 00:11:36,322
Loyalty. Doctor Evelyn Marsh: seventy-eight per cent,
to ninety-six. This one now believes

151
00:11:36,341 --> 00:11:42,317
in you rather than in the work. The
distinction will matter later.

152
00:11:42,907 --> 00:11:48,761
He put the glasses back on and went up to the sixth
floor, and read that note four times,

153
00:11:48,781 --> 00:11:54,438
and — because he was Albert Wesker, and because it
was the single most useful sentence

154
00:11:54,458 --> 00:12:00,180
the System had ever given him free of charge — filed
it under the heading of *things to

155
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:02,557
be careful of in about seven years*.

156
00:12:02,956 --> 00:12:04,257
He was two years out.
